.TH CLRUCACHE_CREATE_FULL 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clrucache_create_full \- create an LRU cache with full control
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clrucache.h>

clru_cache clrucache_create_full(size_t capacity,
                                 ccol_data_type key_type,
                                 ccol_data_type val_type,
                                 clru_remote_getter_t getter,
                                 clru_remote_setter_t setter,
                                 clru_eviction_cb_t eviction_cb,
                                 ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                                 char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a thread-safe LRU cache backed by a hash map (O(1) lookup) and
a doubly-linked list (O(1) eviction). The cache holds up to capacity
live entries; inserting beyond that limit evicts the least-recently-used
entry first.

The cache is divided into independently locked segments, and a key
belongs to exactly one of them. Threads working on keys in different
segments do not wait for one another, so throughput holds up as threads
are added. Eviction is per segment; see NOTES.

This is the low-level constructor. Prefer the clru_construct() macro
family in application code.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
capacity     Maximum number of live entries before eviction occurs.
             Must be > 0.
key_type     ccol_data_type of keys, as returned by
             ccol_determine_ccol_data_type().
val_type     ccol_data_type of values (used for type tracking only;
             the internal map always stores heap pointers).
getter       Remote getter callback, or NULL. Called on cache miss to
             fetch the value from an external source. On success it
             must heap-allocate the value data, assign it to val->ptr,
             set val->size to the byte length, and return true. The
             cache takes ownership of val->ptr and frees it with its
             own allocator (mprocs if one was given, or malloc()/
             free() otherwise); the getter's allocation must use that
             same allocator or the cache's eventual free will corrupt
             the heap. On failure it must leave val->ptr as NULL and
             return false.
setter       Remote setter callback, or NULL. Called synchronously
             before each cache update. Receives the key and value as
             const cmap_pair* pointers. If it returns false the cache
             is not updated.
eviction_cb  Eviction callback, or NULL. Called while the owning
             segment's lock is held when an entry is evicted to make
             room. Receives the evicted key and value as const
             cmap_pair* pointers. Must not call back into the cache,
             and is not serialised against itself: see NOTES.
mprocs       Custom memory management procedures, or NULL for default
             malloc/free.
err          Optional pointer to receive an error string on failure.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new cache handle on success.
Returns CLRU_CACHE_INVALID on failure; *err is set if err is
non-NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The returned handle is an opaque VALUE (a packed {slot index,
generation} pair), not a pointer; never cast it to/from void* or
treat it as an address.
Thread-safe: operations on the returned handle are protected by
per-segment locks and per-entry condition variables. Operations on the
same key still serialise against each other, which is what the
coalescing of concurrent misses for one key rests on.

Splitting begins at a capacity of 128, the first that gives two segments
64 entries each. Below that a cache is a single segment and evicts in one
exact global least-recently-used order.

Above that size eviction is per segment. capacity is divided across the
segments, and each evicts its own least-recently-used entry when its
own share is full, so the cache is sized for the capacity requested (and
clrucache_capacity() reports exactly that) while the entry chosen to
make room is the oldest within one segment rather than the oldest
overall. Because keys are spread by hash, some segments run fuller than
others at any instant, so a cache filled to exactly its capacity holds a
little less than that number. The shortfall is a few percent: up to
about ten below roughly a thousand entries, and around two at eight
thousand and above, falling further as the cache grows. It does not
depend on the key type or on the width of the build.
Benchmark the configuration against
the intended access pattern rather than assuming either behaviour.

The eviction callback is invoked while the owning segment's lock is
held; it must not re-enter the cache. Only that one segment's lock is
held, so two evictions in different segments run the callback
concurrently on two threads. Any state the callback keeps of its own
needs its own protection.
__clrucache_destroy waits for every in-flight resolved use of the
handle (a concurrently executing clrucache_get_full/_set_full/
clrucache_size/_capacity call) to finish before freeing anything, so
the caller does not need to independently prove no other thread is
still using the cache; see __clrucache_destroy(3).
.fi
